s="RatingStar__fill"></path></svg></span></span></div><span class="Text Text__body3"><a href="https://www.goodreads.com/review/show/8446745273">April 26, 2026</a></span></section><section class="ReviewText ReviewText--hideContent"><section class="ReviewText__content" dir="auto"><div class="TruncatedContent" tabindex="-1"><div class="TruncatedContent__text TruncatedContent__text--large" tabindex="-1" data-testid="contentContainer"><span class="Formatted"><blockquote>
  <b>"As someone who <i>is</i> a murderer, I say with the utmost confidence that Ahnna didn't do this. She's being framed, and my gut tells me that Alexandra is at the center of it."</b>
</blockquote><br /><br />Can all books be about Lara Veliant?<br /><br />Genuinely. Because I fucking love her. And she proves CONSTANTLY how much that love is warranted. She’s not just a badass, she’s also so fucking smart she saw through Alexandra’s frame job instantly. Some may call her overpowered; I call her the best fmc I’ve ever read. Argue with the wall.<br /><br />I’m pleased to note, as an aside, that I’m also significantly more fond of Keris in this book than I have been previously. So that’s a major plus because bro has hella screen time—it would have been intolerable if I hated him.<br /><br />But seriously, you can tell he’s the author’s favorite character. We spend so much unnecessary time in his POV, and it’s all very clearly just fan service. His chapters (excessive in number and needlessly lengthy) weigh the plot down and make the story <i>drag.</i><br /><br />I can’t deny that they’re ultimately worth it because we also get Lara and Aren’s POVs. And I might never see them again so I’ll take what I can get. But painstakingly, I must confess that their POVs contribute to the sluggish pace. Most of it would have worked better as a bonus novella, leaving this book to focus on Ahnna and James’s story. Because by the last like 20%, while I simultaneously didn’t want to part with Lara and Aren forever, I was more than ready for the book to just end already.<br /><br />Anyway.<br /><br />When it came to Aren hunting down Lara, there was never a single moment where I even half believed that when he found her, he could kill her. With James, it’s much different. Because the romance was so underdeveloped in the previous books, I have no difficulty—or even a hint of a problem—accepting the idea that James would kill her the second he found her. That being a false belief—if he could kill her, it wouldn’t be much of a romance—doesn’t help much, because I don’t feel any emotional weight holding him back, and I should.<br /><br /><blockquote>
  <b>He cast his eyes skyward. "Keep this up, Princess, and you'll make Carlo's job easier."<br />"Kiss my ass."<br />"Bend over."</b>
</blockquote><br /><br />I know this is supposed to come off as playful banter, but after everything he did, I’m irritated by his audacity, not charmed. He should be groveling in guilt, not acting so shameless and sure of himself. More frustrating still, that comment made her blush. I really hate when a romance skips steps because they just <i>can’t resist</i> each other.<br /><br />They’re kissing and he’s telling her he wants her and loves her, and she’s saying they can’t be together because they stand on different sides of the war. They should be confronting the remnants of betrayal that linger between them. He should be thinking about how badly he hurt her (before <i>and</i> after his father was killed) instead of her long, long legs, and then he should be giving her an apology he actually means.<br /><br /><b>Can you believe Ahnna is the first (and only) one to apologize?</b> Absolutely ridiculous.<br /><br /><blockquote>
  <b>Aren had not missed the mark in his assessment of James's sentiment when he'd crossed paths with the pair in Verwyrd, but it struck Lara that what had been between the two then had grown tenfold in the time since. Love, sure and true, and it hurt Lara's own heart to know that both would suffer for it.</b>
</blockquote><br /><br />The story constantly reaffirms how undeniably in love Ahnna and James are, but I fear I fail to see what Lara sees. I just think their relationship needed to be more emotionally fleshed out than it was for me to really believe what the book was trying to sell me. This could have been a five-star read. I almost thought it would be.<br /><br />I already touched on this, but I still think we got past the him-hunting-her-down-without-hearing-her-out part way too easily. Being captured and tortured will do that to you, I guess (lol), but at least a conversation would have been nice.<br /><br />That’s not to say I wasn’t invested, because I was. I think they complemented each other quite well, and there was a lot about their relationship that I loved, like how he stood up to Aren for her. I just wanted more than what I got.<br /><br /><b>Other brief side notes:</b><br /><br />* I was so happy with Ahnna and Lara’s reconciliation scene. Now that I have their sisterhood, I’m mad I didn’t have it for longer.<br />* I was so right in my review of the last book to say that I hate Georgie and Ahnna should have just killed him. She would have saved everybody a whole lot of grief.<br />* The plot with the warring kingdoms and all their moving parts finally coming together was done very well, and resulted in a wonderfully satisfying conclusion. The plot armor was definitely shiny, but I didn’t really mind.<br />* I loved the undercurrent of Sarhina dissolving the Maridrinian monarchy in favor of an elected government. It’s a small hint at meaningful reform, which I really enjoyed. Jensen is great at layering complex political dynamics beneath the surface of her narratives, and it truly elevates them to a whole new level.<br />* Can this bitch Lestara just die?<br />* The scene where Aren saved Ahnna from the gallows with his flawless aim was <i>incredible.</i><br />* The epilogue was EVVEERRYTHINGGG, I loved absolutely every second of it<br />* It makes me giddy on the inside that Aren is slightly taller than James hahahaha<br />* Did Nana seriously have to die bruh<br /><br />Also: <br />I was eating dinner when I got to the scene with that weird psycho Carlo dude kissing Ahnna on the cheek every two seconds, and I literally had to stop eating until it was over because it was making me nauseous.<br /><br />What does it say about me that Carlo’s death was like the most distressing moment of this book for me? I wrote my reaction above in the moment, after which I found myself growing accidentally fond of Carlo’s deranged ass and his absolutely unhinged behavior.<br /><br />I was holding out hope for his survival until the very end—when James slit his throat—and even then I waited an extra beat for the book to pull a last-second fake-out. He would have made life quite difficult for our main characters, so I understand why he had to die. But I’m still mourning him. :(<br /><br />- - - - -<br />Imagine my utter shock and subsequent delight when I opened the table of contents to find that Lara &amp; Aren narrate a significant portion of this.<br /><br />Unfortunately, there’s also Keris’s perspective, which I will <s>gladly</s> suffer through for LAREN MY BABIES</span></div><div class=""></div></div></section><div class="Alert Alert--informational">This entire review has been hidden because of spoilers.<div class="Alert__footer"><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium" aria-label="Activate this button to show the hidden review."><span class="Button__labelItem">Show full review</span></button></div></div></div></section><section class="ReviewCard__tags"><ul class="CollapsableList"><span tabindex="-1"></span><span tabindex="-1"></span></ul></section><footer class="SocialFooter"><div data-testid="stats" class="SocialFooter__statsContainer"><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium Button--subdued"><span class="Butt